---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---

<Base currentPath="/" title="Urma Protocol">
  <!-- Hero -->
  <header class="relative py-20 border-b border-border mb-20 overflow-hidden">
    <div class="relative z-10 max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-6xl font-medium tracking-[-0.02em] leading-[1.1] text-ink mb-8">
        A protocol for mirroring content across independent networks.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg leading-[1.65] text-on-surface-variant max-w-2xl border-l border-border pl-4">
        A claims-based protocol for tracing files through a web of mirrors. Connection, not storage.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- Connection Model -->
  <section class="mb-20 prose">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-8">
      <span class="w-8 h-px bg-border"></span>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl font-medium text-ink">
        The Connection Model
      </h3>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-6">
      <div class="md:col-span-8 space-y-6">
        <p class="text-lg leading-[1.65] text-ink">
          The philosophy of Urma is built entirely on the concept of the <strong>Connection Model</strong>.
          Rather than concerning itself with where data is stored or how it is transmitted, Urma focuses
          purely on providing a censorship-resistant link from a claim to a storage mirror.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink">
          A claim name resolves to one or more mirror locations: alternative storage sources for the
          same content. Each mirror is an independent copy on a different storage network. Urma does not
          verify that mirrors are byte-identical across networks. It provides the discovery mechanism:
          given a claim, find its mirrors. Verification happens at download time, per blob, by hash.
        </p>

        <Callout title="Note">
          Urma does not store data. It stores <em>claims about data</em>. It is a metadata layer, not a
          storage layer.
        </Callout>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-4 pl-4 border-l border-border hidden md:block">
        <ul class="font-mono text-sm text-muted space-y-4">
          <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-border"></span>
            Storage Agnostic
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-border"></span>
            Verifiable Claims
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-border"></span>
            Decentralized Topology
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-border"></span>
            Proof-Based Integrity
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section divider -->
  <div class="section-divider">
    <div class="star"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- How It Works -->
  <section class="mb-20 bg-paper-alt p-8 md:p-12 border border-border">
    <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl font-medium text-ink mb-6">
      How It Works
    </h3>
    <p class="text-ink mb-8 max-w-2xl">
      The protocol operates through a chain of signed claims and manifests. A publisher declares that
      a mirror exists for a piece of content, stakes a claim on the network, and broadcasts it. No
      one verifies the claim at broadcast time, like a torrent, the claim is an assertion. Staking
      is an anti-spam mechanism and trust signal: it costs something to publish, which
      raises the bar for malicious actors. Actual verification happens at download time, per blob,
      by hash.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="bg-paper border border-border p-6">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
          <span class="star-marker"></span>
          <span class="label-caps text-origin">Step 01</span>
        </div>
        <h4 class="font-serif text-lg font-medium text-ink mb-2">Publish</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            A publisher mirrors content on a storage network, constructs the manifest pages and root
            pointer (backend-specific), and wraps them in an UrmaClaim. Staking the claim on-chain
            costs something: an anti-spam mechanism and trust signal.
          </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-paper border border-border p-6">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-thread"></span>
          <span class="label-caps text-thread">Step 02</span>
        </div>
        <h4 class="font-serif text-lg font-medium text-ink mb-2">Discover</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Clients compute the deterministic Urma name from a source claim ID and query the claimtrie
          for all mirrors at that name.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-paper border border-border p-6">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-muted"></span>
          <span class="label-caps text-muted">Step 03</span>
        </div>
        <h4 class="font-serif text-lg font-medium text-ink mb-2">Verify</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Each blob is verified via SHA-384 hash comparison. Signatures are checked against the
          channel public key. Poisoned mirrors fail on first download.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Naming -->
  <section class="mb-20 prose">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-8">
      <span class="w-8 h-px bg-border"></span>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl font-medium text-ink">
        Claim Naming
      </h3>
    </div>

    <p class="text-ink max-w-[75ch] mb-6">
      Every Urma claim is identified by a deterministic name derived from the source content's ClaimID.
      The format is simple but precise:
    </p>

    <div class="bg-ink text-code-text rounded p-4 inline-block mb-6">
      <code class="font-mono text-sm">u-&#123;source_claim_id_hex&#125;</code>
    </div>

    <p class="text-ink max-w-[75ch]">
      The <code>u-</code> prefix designates the resource as a native Urma entity. The payload is a
      40-character lowercase hex encoding of the 20-byte source ClaimID. Clients compute this name
      from content they already discovered, then query for all Urma claims at that name.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
